1
00:00:04,639 --> 00:00:05,680
What's up nerds?

2
00:00:05,679 --> 00:00:08,240
I'm ***, the first instructor for the ***
Dev course.

3
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:10,337
In order for us to get the best out of our
agents,

4
00:00:10,337 --> 00:00:12,560
we need to understand how the agents
themselves work.

5
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:15,919
And that's why we're starting with lesson
one, generating text.

6
00:00:15,919 --> 00:00:17,600
I know this might seem pretty basic,

7
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,168
but hear me out because there's a lot of
misconceptions

8
00:00:20,168 --> 00:00:24,043
that I would go as far as to say lies, that
we just kind of make assumptions around

9
00:00:24,043 --> 00:00:26,331
and believe because it's easier to work that
way,

10
00:00:26,331 --> 00:00:28,525
but I think understanding the details will
make

11
00:00:28,525 --> 00:00:30,720
it easier to use these models more
effectively.

12
00:00:30,719 --> 00:00:32,307
When you send a message to a model,

13
00:00:32,307 --> 00:00:35,119
it's important to understand what it gets
and how it responds.

14
00:00:35,119 --> 00:00:36,479
It's not as simple as you think.

15
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:39,504
You're used to sending a text to a person
that they read, they think about,

16
00:00:39,504 --> 00:00:41,520
they look into some things, and then they
respond.

17
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:43,199
LLMs don't work that way.

18
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,000
We make them act that way, but that's not
how they work underneath.

19
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,582
The simplest way of explaining it is that
the model

20
00:00:48,582 --> 00:00:52,024
makes informed guesses based on the
information in the chat history,

21
00:00:52,024 --> 00:00:54,960
and it does this chunk by chunk, not
response by response.

22
00:00:54,960 --> 00:00:58,240
It's not generating the perfect message
based on the history,

23
00:00:58,240 --> 00:01:01,519
it's generating the perfect next token based
on prior tokens.

24
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:03,760
Let's start with a really simple first
message.

25
00:01:03,759 --> 00:01:04,879
Subnerds.

26
00:01:04,879 --> 00:01:08,159
This message would be read by a person word
by word.

27
00:01:08,159 --> 00:01:11,039
LLMs don't see words though, they see
tokens.

28
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:13,657
Tokens are the chunks of text that LLMs use

29
00:01:13,657 --> 00:01:18,343
to understand and make suggestions as to
what comes next, because in the end,

30
00:01:18,343 --> 00:01:22,725
LLMs are effectively just autocomplete they
generate new tokens based on

31
00:01:22,725 --> 00:01:26,924
the previous token for example let's take
the sentence the capital of

32
00:01:26,924 --> 00:01:29,906
the US is blank if you hand this to a
traditional

33
00:01:29,906 --> 00:01:33,923
completion API with an LLM it would then
generate the next tokens,

34
00:01:33,923 --> 00:01:36,480
which in this case would be Washington DC.

35
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:41,204
Notice how this is breaking things up
though, with the dots, the spaces,

36
00:01:41,204 --> 00:01:44,879
and the letters sometimes being included,
sometimes not.

37
00:01:44,879 --> 00:01:47,860
These are all details of how different
tokenizers are implemented,

38
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:50,480
and certain things work well for code,
certain ones don't.

39
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:54,799
A ton of iteration and innovation has
happened in tokenizers over the last few
years.

40
00:01:54,799 --> 00:01:57,629
None of that is super important to
understand beyond the

41
00:01:57,629 --> 00:02:01,066
fact that models aren't seeing the specific
paragraphs we send them,

42
00:02:01,066 --> 00:02:04,149
they're getting these chunks of text and
then guessing what's

43
00:02:04,149 --> 00:02:06,879
most likely to be next based on what we've
given them.

44
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:09,680
Let's see how this looks in action with the
simple demo with autocomplete.

45
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:12,672
I'm intentionally using an old and kind of
dumb model here,

46
00:02:12,672 --> 00:02:16,880
but thankfully can actually run on my
computer to show the insides of how it
works.

47
00:02:16,879 --> 00:02:18,834
Let's say we send a message like,

48
00:02:18,834 --> 00:02:23,039
write a JavaScript function that checks if a
number is even, only code.

49
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:25,439
The expectation is that we get back code.

50
00:02:25,439 --> 00:02:27,759
So let's see how the model actually does
that.

51
00:02:27,759 --> 00:02:30,800
The first important thing to understand is
what the model actually gets.

52
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:34,079
It's not getting the text history, it's not
getting a bunch of tokens,

53
00:02:34,079 --> 00:02:37,030
it's getting a specific text format that
describes the state of

54
00:02:37,030 --> 00:02:39,840
the conversation and sets the model up to do
the next thing.

55
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:42,320
The format for the text here is called chat
ML.

56
00:02:42,319 --> 00:02:45,408
There's a bunch of new formats since OpenAI
since Open Sourced Harmony,

57
00:02:45,408 --> 00:02:46,800
which is a pretty good standard.

58
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:49,120
The thing to notice here though is these
call-outs.

59
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:52,080
The IM underscore start and I am underscore
end.

60
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:54,876
These are special tokens that the models are
aware of because they're

61
00:02:54,876 --> 00:02:58,159
used during training that allow different
behaviors to happen at different times.

62
00:02:58,159 --> 00:03:00,977
Like for a user to send a message, the
system to send a message,

63
00:03:00,977 --> 00:03:03,840
and then for the agent or assistant to
respond to these messages.

64
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:07,192
So while we send something that looks like
traditional JSON

65
00:03:07,192 --> 00:03:11,680
wrapped in an array to the model over the
API, it doesn't get these JSON blobs.

66
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:14,800
It instead gets this custom format that it's
using during training.

67
00:03:15,039 --> 00:03:16,159
The format is pretty simple.

68
00:03:16,159 --> 00:03:18,719
We have the indicator of a new message
start.

69
00:03:18,719 --> 00:03:22,800
It immediately opens with the type of
message, whether it's system, user, or
assistant.

70
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:26,800
We then give more context, in this case that
it is Quen, it is a helpful assistant.

71
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:30,240
We then have a new message after the end,
which is a user message.

72
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:32,800
This indicates that a user is sending this
thing.

73
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:35,840
And then the most important part, we hard
code a start.

74
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:38,800
We immediately say I am underscore start
assistant.

75
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:41,040
This is important because it's an
autocomplete model.

76
00:03:41,039 --> 00:03:42,809
It has to generate the next token,

77
00:03:42,809 --> 00:03:46,400
so it wouldn't really know what to do if it
just ended the user turn.

78
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:48,893
So we hard code a start to the next turn so

79
00:03:48,893 --> 00:03:52,255
the model can do what it thinks makes the
most sense next,

80
00:03:52,255 --> 00:03:54,400
which we can see when we press enter.

81
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:58,960
Now that we have run this, we can see how
the model responded with these percentages.

82
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:00,560
The percentages might be confusing.

83
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:03,446
The simplest way I can explain it is these
percentages show how

84
00:04:03,446 --> 00:04:06,240
confident the model is with the given pick
for the next term.

85
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:09,044
Here it thinks that after our message asking
it to

86
00:04:09,044 --> 00:04:12,217
write a JavaScript function that checks if a
number is even,

87
00:04:12,217 --> 00:04:15,920
that the best next response is the word
certainly with 66% confidence.

88
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:18,880
Next it thinks sure it's a good option of
14%.

89
00:04:18,879 --> 00:04:22,960
Next, it thinks that three back ticks could
be a good option with 14% as well.

90
00:04:22,959 --> 00:04:26,116
This is the option I would have preferred
since we told it to only send code,

91
00:04:26,116 --> 00:04:29,600
but it wanted to be a helpful assistant,
which steered it towards the word certainly.

92
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:32,530
Next, it was relatively confident, like 92%
confident,

93
00:04:32,530 --> 00:04:35,840
that an exclamation point was the best next
thing to include.

94
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:37,600
Then it suggested below.

95
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:39,279
This little dot is for a space.

96
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:41,199
So next I thought space below made sense.

97
00:04:41,199 --> 00:04:43,040
Then I thought space is made sense.

98
00:04:43,519 --> 00:04:45,864
yada yada and you can see all the other
things

99
00:04:45,864 --> 00:04:49,280
it was considering before it responded with
these particular words.

100
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:52,240
It then continued generating and finished
its response.

101
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:54,789
And at the very end, if we had let it go all
the way,

102
00:04:54,789 --> 00:04:57,626
it would hopefully have ended with one of
these end tokens,

103
00:04:57,626 --> 00:05:01,280
thereby signifying to whatever tool is
running this that the model can stop.

104
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:03,199
Remember, the model has to stop eventually.

105
00:05:03,199 --> 00:05:04,800
It can't just autocomplete forever.

106
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:08,080
So one of the things they're trained on is
this idea of stops.

107
00:05:08,079 --> 00:05:11,600
They can respond with a certain phrase to
trigger the end.

108
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:13,120
That's not the only thing they can trigger.

109
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:14,400
Remember this, we'll go back to it.

110
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:18,347
One more small thing to showcase the
non-deterministic nature of all this,

111
00:05:18,347 --> 00:05:20,907
but the weird patterns that happen
underneath is

112
00:05:20,907 --> 00:05:23,360
asking Quantum 25 to generate a random
number.

113
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:28,879
It picks 8 with 20% confidence, 7 with 18%,
9 of 16%, etc.

114
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:31,840
So its definition of random is not quite our
definition.

115
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:34,320
One more call out about the generation that
it did here.

116
00:05:34,319 --> 00:05:38,720
You'll see that when it said console.log is
even seven, it responded true.

117
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:43,520
That is not a good response, because as I
hope you understand, seven isn't even.

118
00:05:43,519 --> 00:05:47,245
The problem with this style of generation is
that the model can't go back and

119
00:05:47,245 --> 00:05:51,600
correct itself because it's just outputting
one token at a time appending to this
history.

120
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:53,920
It can't go back and say that's wrong and
edit it.

121
00:05:53,920 --> 00:05:56,800
It has to just keep adding to the existing
text corpus.

122
00:05:56,800 --> 00:05:58,872
This style of autocomplete responses allows
for

123
00:05:58,872 --> 00:06:01,120
models to share information that they
already know.

124
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:03,914
So if you ask the model who was president in
1952,

125
00:06:03,914 --> 00:06:08,160
it's able to generate a response based on
the information it was trained on.

126
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:11,922
But then if you ask a modern smart model
like Opus 5.5 what's today's date,

127
00:06:11,922 --> 00:06:14,079
you're not going to get a very good answer.

128
00:06:14,079 --> 00:06:16,880
It's actually going to answer hundreds of
days behind.

129
00:06:16,879 --> 00:06:19,384
If you then ask it what the latest version
of a package is,

130
00:06:19,384 --> 00:06:23,120
you're never going to get a correct answer,
assuming it's been updated anytime recently.

131
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:26,399
All of this is because this data has changed
since the model was trained,

132
00:06:26,399 --> 00:06:28,240
or maybe it was just trained on old data.

133
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:30,588
But when we use models and tools like Claude
Code,

134
00:06:30,588 --> 00:06:32,560
we don't see all of these types of issues.

135
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:36,891
The reason is because the model can get that
data other ways, not through its training,

136
00:06:36,891 --> 00:06:39,280
not through its intelligence, but through
tools.

137
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:41,127
In order to understand how agents get data,

138
00:06:41,127 --> 00:06:43,920
we have to do something that a lot of us
haven't done in a while.

139
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:44,879
Read some code.

140
00:06:44,879 --> 00:06:47,840
Jokes aside, I think it's important to
understand all of this.

141
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:52,399
I have some code here that allows a model
response to be parsed and then run as bash.

142
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:55,520
Remember what I said earlier about the model
having to indicate when to stop?

143
00:06:55,519 --> 00:06:58,971
One of the reasons it wants to stop isn't
just to finish responding,

144
00:06:58,971 --> 00:07:02,880
it's to generate a tool call that allows it
to get data that it doesn't know.

145
00:07:02,879 --> 00:07:05,044
Like if it wants to know what the current
date is,

146
00:07:05,044 --> 00:07:07,339
if it wants to know what files are in your
code base,

147
00:07:07,339 --> 00:07:09,721
if it wants to know what packages are
available on npm,

148
00:07:09,721 --> 00:07:12,320
the model has tools it can use to do that in
most harnesses.

149
00:07:12,319 --> 00:07:15,248
And those tools have custom syntaxes the
model can use in

150
00:07:15,248 --> 00:07:18,640
order to respond and trigger those types of
behaviors and actions.

151
00:07:18,639 --> 00:07:22,560
In order for this to work, you need to stop
generating once one of these tokens comes
in.

152
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:28,000
In this case, we have a simple example where
stop sequence includes closing tag for bash.

153
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,251
What we're saying here is when we see this
in the response,

154
00:07:31,251 --> 00:07:33,840
that means stop and we have to go do
something.

155
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:37,927
So the model will send something along the
lines of bash ls close bash,

156
00:07:37,927 --> 00:07:40,000
which allows it to run this command.

157
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:41,520
And our code is pretty simple here.

158
00:07:41,519 --> 00:07:44,160
We grab the command out of that syntax.

159
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:48,723
If we don't have a command, we just respond
with the text, but if we do have a command,

160
00:07:48,723 --> 00:07:51,083
we output the command and then we run it
with

161
00:07:51,083 --> 00:07:53,600
this sh helper to get the output of the
command.

162
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:56,800
And then importantly, we push this onto the
messages.

163
00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:57,920
We'll talk about that in just a bit.

164
00:07:57,920 --> 00:08:01,200
So let's see this in action with a simple
demo that I just threw together.

165
00:08:01,199 --> 00:08:05,098
The user sends what's in this directory and
the model responds, oh,

166
00:08:05,098 --> 00:08:08,240
I can't see your file system, but it
absolutely could.

167
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:11,360
If it just responded with that format, it
would work fine.

168
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:12,640
So what's up?

169
00:08:12,639 --> 00:08:15,120
Why can't it see this data?

170
00:08:15,120 --> 00:08:17,040
The reason is actually quite simple.

171
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:20,480
We didn't tell the model that it has access
to this syntax.

172
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:23,840
We can't just give it a tool and expect it
to know how to use it.

173
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:27,120
We have to tell it what the tool is and that
it exists in the first place.

174
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:31,200
Otherwise it has no idea that certain types
of text can trigger certain behaviors.

175
00:08:31,199 --> 00:08:32,399
Here we have done just that.

176
00:08:32,399 --> 00:08:35,002
We added to the system prompt this tool
prompt,

177
00:08:35,002 --> 00:08:37,494
which describes that the model is able to
get

178
00:08:37,494 --> 00:08:40,319
data by using this format in order to run
commands.

179
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:43,494
So if we rerun this with this addition to
the system prompt,

180
00:08:43,494 --> 00:08:46,827
you'll see the model will run these
different commands in order

181
00:08:46,827 --> 00:08:50,319
to figure out what's in this project and
then respond accordingly.

182
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:54,000
This is a small JavaScript program called
Cart, a shopping cart pricing module.

183
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:55,680
yada, you get the idea.

184
00:08:55,680 --> 00:08:59,360
It's able to do all of this now because it
knows that it has this tool it can call.

185
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:00,960
And the bash tool is pretty powerful.

186
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:03,439
It gives the model the ability to do a lot
of different things.

187
00:09:03,440 --> 00:09:07,919
This also showcases the importance of the
system prompt and how it steers model
behavior.

188
00:09:07,919 --> 00:09:11,415
When the model has the ability to trigger
certain behaviors or special syntaxes,

189
00:09:11,415 --> 00:09:13,600
it needs to know about it and know what it
can do.

190
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:15,110
And then once it does those things,

191
00:09:15,110 --> 00:09:18,000
it needs to get the results back so that it
can continue operating.

192
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,480
This all starts with what we usually call a
system prompt.

193
00:09:20,480 --> 00:09:23,199
In this case I'll call it system message
because there can be more than one.

194
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:25,436
In a simple example I tell the model that it

195
00:09:25,436 --> 00:09:28,079
can use bash to get info using the following
syntax.

196
00:09:28,080 --> 00:09:32,639
We then follow up with a simple user message
that says, what files are in this project?

197
00:09:32,639 --> 00:09:34,260
So when it is spun up to respond,

198
00:09:34,260 --> 00:09:38,240
it can take advantage of all of this data
which steers it in a certain direction.

199
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:42,480
One of the things the model can do is think,
at least if it's a modern reasoning model.

200
00:09:42,480 --> 00:09:45,840
So it will think about what it has access
to, what is being asked for.

201
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:48,480
And remember earlier when I said the model
can't correct itself?

202
00:09:48,480 --> 00:09:50,091
It can't if it says something wrong,

203
00:09:50,091 --> 00:09:53,716
but if it's talking to itself in the
thinking traces and it gets something wrong,

204
00:09:53,716 --> 00:09:57,519
it can correct itself and just say, no,
that's not right, I should go this other
way.

205
00:09:57,519 --> 00:10:00,960
So by the time it gets to the output stage,
it's much more likely to be correct.

206
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:05,360
So after it thinks for a bit, it says, good
question, let me check what files are here.

207
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:07,279
And then it runs this bash command.

208
00:10:07,279 --> 00:10:09,039
But then I have this line drawn.

209
00:10:09,039 --> 00:10:11,680
I have this line drawn for a very important
reason.

210
00:10:11,680 --> 00:10:13,200
We're done.

211
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:18,768
After this response comes through and we hit
our end token, which is this close bash,

212
00:10:18,768 --> 00:10:20,799
that is the end of the request.

213
00:10:20,639 --> 00:10:23,679
the API request that was made is over.

214
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:27,519
The reason is because something has to
happen before it can continue.

215
00:10:27,519 --> 00:10:32,079
We asked for this command to be run, so now
we have to go run said command.

216
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:35,421
Usually what this means is whatever's
running this assistant,

217
00:10:35,421 --> 00:10:39,858
probably like a Claude Code type tool, will
fire off the command, get a response,

218
00:10:39,858 --> 00:10:43,199
and then send that response back as an
append to the history.

219
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:46,179
Every time an API call is made to the Claude
Code servers, Codex,

220
00:10:46,179 --> 00:10:50,166
whatever else you're using, it usually will
include the entire chat history because the

221
00:10:50,166 --> 00:10:52,640
model needs that in order to generate the
next tokens.

222
00:10:52,639 --> 00:10:56,974
And when a tool call is made, the important
thing to understand is that the response

223
00:10:56,974 --> 00:10:59,760
is run on whatever computer the harness is
running on,

224
00:10:59,760 --> 00:11:01,618
and that response is appended to the

225
00:11:01,618 --> 00:11:04,559
history so the model can continue generating
accordingly.

226
00:11:04,560 --> 00:11:07,102
The same way that you send a question to the
model,

227
00:11:07,102 --> 00:11:11,040
the tool call sends a response to the model,
which allows it to continue after.

228
00:11:11,039 --> 00:11:12,955
And the model then decides when to stop,

229
00:11:12,955 --> 00:11:16,165
whether it's stopping for a tool to be run
or it's stopping because

230
00:11:16,165 --> 00:11:19,279
it's done doing whatever it has to do and it
wants your response.

231
00:11:19,279 --> 00:11:21,161
The tool or the model chooses when to stop

232
00:11:21,161 --> 00:11:23,759
with one of these indicators and then you
act accordingly.

233
00:11:23,759 --> 00:11:27,199
Let's talk a bit more about this part quick,
the system message.

234
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:29,368
The example I have here is relatively small,

235
00:11:29,368 --> 00:11:32,079
but this is not how things will work in the
real world.

236
00:11:32,080 --> 00:11:34,079
I have another quick demo to show what I
mean.

237
00:11:34,080 --> 00:11:37,543
I'm going to simply say hi to a handful of
different tools, harnesses,

238
00:11:37,543 --> 00:11:39,077
whatever you want to call them,

239
00:11:39,077 --> 00:11:42,639
to see how they respond and specifically how
many tokens are being used.

240
00:11:42,639 --> 00:11:44,741
If I send a simple say hi to Claude,

241
00:11:44,741 --> 00:11:49,120
it's not going to use the three tokens that
say hi would be represented by.

242
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:52,960
It's going to use a hell of a lot more,
thousands even.

243
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:55,541
I have tool search on, which means a lot of
my tools,

244
00:11:55,541 --> 00:11:59,631
MCPs and stuff aren't included by default,
just little bits of info that they exist,

245
00:11:59,631 --> 00:12:03,040
so the model knows they exist to go search
for them with another tool.

246
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:07,840
But it's still 21,000 tokens for what seems
like a very simple request.

247
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:11,360
Say hi, which it responded with, hi ***,
what do you want to work on?

248
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:12,879
Oh, how does it know it's me?

249
00:12:12,879 --> 00:12:15,368
It knows it's me because I have a CLAUDE.md
file

250
00:12:15,368 --> 00:12:19,360
that describes a handful of things that I
wanted to know, including who I am.

251
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:21,665
It also will do things like figure out what
the

252
00:12:21,665 --> 00:12:24,068
current git status is and run a command
before it

253
00:12:24,068 --> 00:12:26,520
sends the request to include that in the
prompt so

254
00:12:26,520 --> 00:12:29,120
the model has all of this data before it
even starts.

255
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:32,480
Let's pull all this together with the next
important thing to understand.

256
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:33,439
Agents.

257
00:12:33,440 --> 00:12:34,639
What is an agent?

258
00:12:34,639 --> 00:12:38,518
The simplest way to put it is that an agent
is some type of tool or system that

259
00:12:38,518 --> 00:12:40,187
allows for an LLM to run in a loop

260
00:12:40,187 --> 00:12:43,919
and make tool calls to get whatever
information it needs, make changes, etc.

261
00:12:44,080 --> 00:12:45,919
The loop is the important part though.

262
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:47,360
It doesn't just stop here.

263
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:50,879
So with our example here, it just stops
after the message runs.

264
00:12:50,879 --> 00:12:53,840
You'd have to go make another API request
for this to continue.

265
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:56,879
When I say that agents are loops, I mean it
quite literally.

266
00:12:56,879 --> 00:13:00,062
Most of the harnesses we use to run agents
are literally

267
00:13:00,062 --> 00:13:02,960
running in a for loop or a while loop of
some form.

268
00:13:02,960 --> 00:13:07,040
In this case we have a simple for loop where
I have a limit for turns at 40.

269
00:13:07,039 --> 00:13:08,960
I'll explain what that means in a sec.

270
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:11,543
I also think this probably should have been
the word step, not turn,

271
00:13:11,543 --> 00:13:12,720
but doesn't really matter here.

272
00:13:12,720 --> 00:13:15,659
This is the first message in the history,
and as you can guess,

273
00:13:15,659 --> 00:13:18,319
we'll be appending new messages to this
history as we go.

274
00:13:18,320 --> 00:13:24,000
So at the start of the loop, we call the
Claude API with a request to create a
message.

275
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:28,096
We say what model, how many tokens we want
it to do at max, the system prompt,
thinking,

276
00:13:28,096 --> 00:13:31,261
which is us turning on thinking saying that
we want to get summaries

277
00:13:31,261 --> 00:13:34,240
of the results and that it's adaptive and
then we give it tools.

278
00:13:34,240 --> 00:13:37,360
In this case we have a simple bash tool that
allows it to run things.

279
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:41,200
We then hand it messages, which is that same
array from the top of the messages.

280
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:43,680
It's arguably the only really important part
here.

281
00:13:43,680 --> 00:13:45,280
Everything else could be kind of changed.

282
00:13:45,279 --> 00:13:46,319
This cannot be changed.

283
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:48,879
The response is what we get back from
Claude.

284
00:13:48,879 --> 00:13:52,080
So at the end here, we push that onto the
history.

285
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:56,148
In this case, we push it as role assistant
because this is a response from the
assistant,

286
00:13:56,148 --> 00:13:58,799
which is the model, and the content is
whatever it's said.

287
00:13:58,879 --> 00:14:00,480
We have to decide what to do next.

288
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:03,440
First we check to see if it is or in this
case isn't tool use.

289
00:14:03,440 --> 00:14:07,012
Because if the model's stopping for a reason
other than calling a tool,

290
00:14:07,012 --> 00:14:08,320
that is because it's done.

291
00:14:08,320 --> 00:14:11,919
Because we've only given it two reasons to
stop, tool calls or being done.

292
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:15,330
So if it is the or so if it's not a tool
call, that means it's done,

293
00:14:15,330 --> 00:14:16,799
so we early return and escape.

294
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:19,920
That means from here on it has to be a tool
call.

295
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:21,679
So now we can assume accordingly.

296
00:14:21,680 --> 00:14:25,365
We grab the calls from response content
filter where we filter

297
00:14:25,365 --> 00:14:27,920
based on if it is a tool call block or not.

298
00:14:27,920 --> 00:14:31,360
This is important because sometimes the
model can respond with more than one tool
call.

299
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:33,879
Here we have results which is where we
collect all

300
00:14:33,879 --> 00:14:36,398
of the results of the things we call because
we're

301
00:14:36,398 --> 00:14:38,867
going to go through and safely parse and
then get

302
00:14:38,867 --> 00:14:41,840
responses for all the different bash
requests that it made.

303
00:14:41,840 --> 00:14:45,603
If any of these fail, we append to results
with an invalid call saying

304
00:14:45,603 --> 00:14:48,399
it's an error and that the content is
invalid input.

305
00:14:48,399 --> 00:14:54,000
Otherwise we rip the data, we run it, we get
an output, and then we send this as a
result.

306
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,960
And at the end here, this is where things
all come together.

307
00:14:56,960 --> 00:15:01,199
We push with role user the contents of those
results.

308
00:15:01,200 --> 00:15:04,198
This means that we're taking all of the
outputs from all of those

309
00:15:04,198 --> 00:15:07,519
tool calls and then appending them to the
history like a normal message.

310
00:15:07,519 --> 00:15:11,254
So what the model would then see is the
results of that tool call

311
00:15:11,254 --> 00:15:15,679
is a bunch of like bash outputs and it knows
to continue working accordingly.

312
00:15:15,680 --> 00:15:19,040
And of course, we can wrap this up with one
last demo.

313
00:15:19,039 --> 00:15:22,080
Pnpm agent will run the code that we were
just looking at.

314
00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:25,600
Here we have a really simple broken demo app
with some tests that are failing.

315
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:27,440
So let's ask the model to fix it.

316
00:15:27,440 --> 00:15:28,799
The tests in this project are failing.

317
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:30,639
Find out why and fix the code.

318
00:15:30,639 --> 00:15:33,083
And here we can see the model responding
with tool calls

319
00:15:33,083 --> 00:15:35,439
that it can run and then it gets the results
of those.

320
00:15:35,440 --> 00:15:38,720
It runs another, it gets the results, and
then it says something.

321
00:15:38,720 --> 00:15:39,439
Two bugs.

322
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:41,600
Discount doesn't divide by 100.

323
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:44,720
It runs another command to edit and fix
that.

324
00:15:44,720 --> 00:15:46,639
It says all five tests pass now.

325
00:15:46,639 --> 00:15:49,759
There were two bugs in Source CartJS and I
didn't touch the tests.

326
00:15:49,759 --> 00:15:51,039
It says what it did.

327
00:15:51,039 --> 00:15:53,946
And then when we run the tests after, they
are correct,

328
00:15:53,946 --> 00:15:57,540
because the model was able to run a command
here, this said command,

329
00:15:57,540 --> 00:15:59,919
to actually edit the files to fix the
issues.

330
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:03,205
So all this agent had was access to bash and
it ran in a for

331
00:16:03,205 --> 00:16:07,257
loop so that every time it did something or
requested a command to be run,

332
00:16:07,257 --> 00:16:09,666
we would run it, append that to the history,

333
00:16:09,666 --> 00:16:12,240
and then let the model generate the next
thing.

334
00:16:12,240 --> 00:16:14,775
Whether that is another tool call, a
explanation,

335
00:16:14,775 --> 00:16:19,120
some code that would be executed via tool
call, all of those things, doesn't matter.

336
00:16:19,120 --> 00:16:23,100
The model is able to keep going until it
tells us to stop or we hit our limit,

337
00:16:23,100 --> 00:16:25,039
which in this case was those 40 steps.

338
00:16:25,040 --> 00:16:27,679
Let's quickly go through the important
takeaways from this lesson.

339
00:16:27,680 --> 00:16:31,439
First and foremost, the models aren't
generating responses to our messages.

340
00:16:31,440 --> 00:16:34,879
They are autocompleting text based on the
existing text history.

341
00:16:34,879 --> 00:16:38,720
If the thing doesn't exist in the history or
the model's weights, it cannot use it.

342
00:16:38,720 --> 00:16:42,799
And this history is helpful in steering the
model towards the right things in its
weights.

343
00:16:42,800 --> 00:16:44,424
If the model is trained properly,

344
00:16:44,424 --> 00:16:48,559
it can use text outputs to request certain
types of data through what we call tools.

345
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:52,177
This allows them to go fetch things that
they might not have, to run commands,

346
00:16:52,177 --> 00:16:54,960
to edit things, and to do stuff other than
just output text.

347
00:16:54,960 --> 00:16:56,128
By doing these tool calls,

348
00:16:56,128 --> 00:16:59,094
it's able to put things in its own history
and make changes in the

349
00:16:59,094 --> 00:17:02,240
world around it in order to make the next
thing it does more accurate.

350
00:17:02,240 --> 00:17:05,600
All of this allows for the model to build
its own context in history.

351
00:17:05,599 --> 00:17:09,600
And the vast majority of the context models
work with are not what we told it.

352
00:17:09,599 --> 00:17:11,427
It's all of the data in the system prompt,

353
00:17:11,427 --> 00:17:13,646
all the tool calls and things that it's
exposed to,

354
00:17:13,646 --> 00:17:16,518
and then it builds the context itself by
using these tool calls to

355
00:17:16,518 --> 00:17:20,000
get the information it needs to make the
next things it generates more accurate.

356
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,059
And by doing this in a loop, you end up with
what we call an agent,

357
00:17:23,059 --> 00:17:25,844
a model that's able to use tool calls to
build the context it

358
00:17:25,844 --> 00:17:29,039
needs and make real changes using all of the
things we just discussed.

359
00:17:29,519 --> 00:17:30,400
That was a lot.

360
00:17:30,400 --> 00:17:32,024
And now that we understand it all,

361
00:17:32,024 --> 00:17:35,893
we can start taking advantage of it by
learning more about the context and how we

362
00:17:35,893 --> 00:17:40,000
can manipulate it to make the models more
successful in the things that we care about.
